WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:05.405
For decades,
people have dreamed

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:08.674
<i> of technology that could
transport them somewhere else--</i>

00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:11.144
<i> a teleporter machine
 that would beam them</i>

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:15.681
from point A to point B
just like that.

00:00:15.714 --> 00:00:19.085
But will this technology
ever actually exist?

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:23.089
And if so,
what would it look like?

00:00:23.122 --> 00:00:25.658
<i> I'm Marques Brownlee,
 and I review dope new tech.</i>

00:00:25.691 --> 00:00:28.061
<i> But on this show,
 I'm rewinding the clock</i>

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:30.063
<i> to look at the tech
 of the past</i>

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:33.499
<i> that we thought
 would be our future.</i>

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:37.103
<i> This is "Retro Tech:
 Teleportation."</i>

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:38.771
Hey, what's up?
MKBHD here.

00:00:38.804 --> 00:00:40.406
So, I'm about
to unbox something.

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:41.674
Don't know what it is.

00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:42.809
There's clearly
not a teleporter

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:44.811
under this desk, right?

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:46.546
Let's figure it out. Okay.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:49.315
Wow, that is a colorful box.

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:52.251
Virtual Boy.
This is a console.

00:00:52.284 --> 00:00:53.586
Interesting.

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:56.722
Virtual Boy, teleporters--

00:00:56.755 --> 00:00:59.325
oh, is this
a virtual reality headset?

00:00:59.358 --> 00:01:01.794
This graphic is
so incredibly '90s,

00:01:01.827 --> 00:01:03.196
it's hard to tell
what's going on,

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.164
but I feel like
I'm seeing a VR headset.

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:07.834
And I guess if you're putting
two and two together,

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.536
teleportation really is, uh,

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:12.772
it's putting you
in a different place.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:16.776
But in 1995,
with a good enough VR headset,

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:18.711
maybe that's the equivalent

00:01:18.744 --> 00:01:20.480
of putting you
in a different place.

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.482
All right,
let's check this out.

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:27.420
Classic '90s packaging here.
Look at all those warnings.

00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:29.188
"Carefully read
this whole thing."

00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:30.857
"Don't put it on your face
if you're less than seven."

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.326
"May cause serious permanent
damage to their vision."

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.728
"May cause epilepsy
or seizures."

00:01:35.761 --> 00:01:37.430
"Repetitive strain injury."

00:01:37.463 --> 00:01:38.865
"Before playing,
consult a doctor."

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.901
Wow. Yeah, that's a lot.
That's a lot.

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:45.738
But, hey, it's red and black.
Not bad, Nintendo.

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:47.774
The tennis game,
I'm looking forward to that.

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:51.210
Batteries go in here.

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:55.481
Close it,
snap it onto the back.

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:59.486
Oh, man, I'd gone so long
without messing something up.

00:02:01.453 --> 00:02:03.756
I remember exactly
how I took it off,

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:05.691
and it doesn't go back on
the same way.

00:02:05.724 --> 00:02:08.261
I'm blowing my own mind
right now.

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:10.363
It should just slide down
these rails.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:11.698
Okay, we got this.

00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:17.336 line:0%
There you go.
Looks like a four-inch display.

00:02:17.369 --> 00:02:20.873 line:0%
All red, dot matrix.
Mario versus Princess.

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:23.276 line:0%
Okay, I've got my backhand.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.278 line:0%
Hit. I hit the ball
back at 'em.

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:26.445 line:0%
The ball's coming back at me.

00:02:26.478 --> 00:02:27.613 line:0%
Oh, I missed,

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:30.716 line:0%
and I am now losing.

00:02:30.749 --> 00:02:32.351
Wow, I'm-- I'm impressed.

00:02:32.384 --> 00:02:34.620
Now, I definitely
don't feel like

00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:37.256
this is teleporting yet,

00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:41.194
but if today's
virtual reality is, like,

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.462
the closest thing
we have to teleportation,

00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:45.398
then this is
the early version of that.

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:46.599
But that's
what we're here out,

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:47.767
so let's get into it.

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:50.803
First thing
I'm gonna ask you to do

00:02:50.836 --> 00:02:51.971
is check underneath
your chair.

00:02:52.004 --> 00:02:53.940 line:0%
Ooh, Oprah moment.

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:56.375 line:0%
All right.

00:02:56.408 --> 00:02:57.977 line:0%
I don't know
what I'm holding right now.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.812 line:0%
- I find it fascinating.
- Uh, these would be--

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:00.813 line:0%
( plays "Star Trek"
teleporter sound )

00:03:00.846 --> 00:03:02.815 line:0%
Oh, it-- it works!

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:06.419 line:0%
I am holding
a miniaturized teleporter.

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:08.654 align:start size:81% position:19%
- That's fantastic.
- Beam me up.

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:10.823
Marques:
So, I guess, let's break down
in its simplest terms,

00:03:10.856 --> 00:03:14.927
what exactly is teleportation?
What is this concept? 

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:16.963
There's that famous expression,
the quickest way

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:19.232
from point A to point B
is a straight line.

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:22.501
And the idea of
teleportation is no line.

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:24.270
Sherri: 
<i> You're being beamed
 at one location,</i>

00:03:24.303 --> 00:03:25.705
<i> being beamed to another.</i>

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:28.307
Like, instantaneous.
Speed of light.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.278 line:0%
It's some sort of a device
that you step into or step on,

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:33.913 line:0%
<i> and then you arrive
 somewhere else,</i>

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:35.982 line:0%
<i> on usually a matching device,</i>

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:38.618 line:0%
<i> where you rematerialize
 on that side.</i>

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.753
There are so many movies
that have been built

00:03:40.786 --> 00:03:44.690 line:0%
around the theory
of teleportation.

00:03:44.723 --> 00:03:47.360 line:0%
Keith:
<i> You have "Power Rangers."
 They're teleporting.</i>

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:50.796 line:0%
But probably
the most iconic representation
of teleportation--

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:52.598
the transporter
in "Star Trek."

00:03:52.631 --> 00:03:55.601 align:start position:0% line:0%
Mr. Spock, prepare
to beam Scotty on board.

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.870 line:0%
"Star Trek" drilled home

00:03:57.903 --> 00:04:00.673 line:0%
<i>that this could be future tech.</i>

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:01.874 line:0%
<i> It's something that we wanted.</i>

00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:03.609 line:0%
Beaming up.
Notify transporter room.

00:04:03.642 --> 00:04:05.411 line:0%
Blake:<i> Sort of around
 the time of "Star Trek,"</i>

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:07.313 line:0%
we had landed on the moon,

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:10.416 line:0%
<i> We felt like we were being
 very ambitious as explorers.</i>

00:04:10.449 --> 00:04:13.719
And the idea of being able
to get someplace fast

00:04:13.752 --> 00:04:15.822
is something that
we're all obsessed with.

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:20.559 line:0%
As a viewer, you hear
all of these science words.

00:04:20.592 --> 00:04:23.062
It seems like a thing
that might be possible.

00:04:23.095 --> 00:04:25.898 line:0%
It uses trionic initiators
in the warp core.

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:28.868 line:0%
Keith:
<i> So who am I, as a 10-year-old
 watching this show,</i>

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.970
<i> not to think, "Yeah, maybe
 they've got it figured out."</i>

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:32.705
Part of the promise
of "Star Trek"

00:04:32.738 --> 00:04:36.042
<i> is believing that we could
 get there someday.</i>

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:38.744
But teleportation
is one of those things that,

00:04:38.777 --> 00:04:42.048
it's so far off as a technology
to be able to achieve,

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:45.184
and those things are possible
only in science fiction.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:52.391
Marques:
<i> So, one of the most important
 aspects of a teleporter</i>

00:04:52.424 --> 00:04:55.761
<i> is it has to completely
 disassemble and reassemble you</i>

00:04:55.794 --> 00:04:59.665
<i> in the exact right place
 every single time.</i>

00:04:59.698 --> 00:05:02.001
<i> But what would it take
 on a physics level</i>

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:04.570
<i> to build a machine
 with that type of accuracy?</i>

00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:06.439
<i> To help me understand
 what's possible,</i>

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:08.074
<i> I'm meeting up with Kevin
 from Vsauce2,</i>

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:09.709
<i> who's known for breaking down
 some of the world's</i>

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:11.944
<i> biggest complexities.</i>

00:05:11.977 --> 00:05:13.579 align:start size:50% position:50%
Kevin, welcome
to "Retro Tech."

00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:15.114 line:0%
Thanks for having me.
I love your place.

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:16.716 align:start size:59% position:41% line:0%
What should we know
about teleporters?

00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:19.652 align:start size:78% position:22%
What is actually possible
right now?

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:21.921 align:start position:0%
Well, we're talking
about teleporting, like,

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:24.957 align:start position:0%
really, really,
really small things,

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.592
<i> like a photon.</i>

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:29.595
<i> That's possible
 with quantum entanglement,</i>

00:05:29.628 --> 00:05:31.931 align:start size:78% position:22%
- which is crazy.
- Fair.

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:34.467 line:0%
Yeah, I imagine
a lot of what we're
seeing in the movies

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.035 line:0%
<i> is either an exaggeration</i>

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:37.870 line:0%
<i> of the little bit
 that's possible</i>

00:05:37.903 --> 00:05:39.505 line:0%
<i> or just completely impossible.</i>

00:05:39.538 --> 00:05:41.974 line:0%
We can't exactly visualize
what it would look like

00:05:42.007 --> 00:05:45.611
if you were dematerialized
down into atoms,

00:05:45.644 --> 00:05:49.382
<i> so let's just pretend
 that you turned into light.</i>

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.517 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Marques:<i> All right. </i>
-<i> It's called the double
  slit experiment.</i>

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.486
<i> It gets complicated.
 Try to stay with me here.</i>

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:57.123
<i> Imagine our teleporter machine
 dematerializes you</i>

00:05:57.156 --> 00:05:59.125 line:0%
<i> into these light photons,</i>

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:01.827 line:0%
<i> and these two slits represent
 the atmosphere pathways</i>

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.496 line:0%
<i> your beamed body
 will travel through</i>

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:05.131 line:0%
<i> on your way
 to your destination.</i>

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:06.699
I am ready to see this.

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:07.900 align:start position:0%
Let's energize.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:14.173
- Huh. That is weird.
- ( Kevin laughs )

00:06:14.206 --> 00:06:19.545
Somehow it turns into
a horizontal string of beads.

00:06:19.578 --> 00:06:21.080
This is called
an interference pattern.

00:06:21.113 --> 00:06:23.682
First of all, if you were
to get into a teleporter,

00:06:23.715 --> 00:06:26.685
we don't want you to be
that series of beads.

00:06:26.718 --> 00:06:29.121
It's not gonna be
a great result for you.

00:06:29.154 --> 00:06:31.023 line:0%
<i> If you were a light beam
 transported,</i>

00:06:31.056 --> 00:06:33.159 line:0%
<i> you might end up
 in a scattered mess,</i>

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:35.161 line:0%
<i> which would be bad.</i>

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:38.030 line:0%
<i> But atoms act
 differently than light,</i>

00:06:38.063 --> 00:06:41.033 line:0%
<i> so in theory your atoms
 will be less scattered</i>

00:06:41.066 --> 00:06:42.701 line:0%
<i> going through those pathways</i>

00:06:42.734 --> 00:06:44.870 line:0%
<i> and easier
 to put back together.</i>

00:06:44.903 --> 00:06:48.040 line:0%
<i> But when scientists did
the same experiment with atoms,</i>

00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:49.809 line:0%
<i> the stuff you
 and I are made of,</i>

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:52.878 line:0%
<i> they found atoms
 weirdly behaved the same way.</i>

00:06:52.911 --> 00:06:54.880 line:0%
<i> They weren't sure
 why this was happening,</i>

00:06:54.913 --> 00:06:57.983 line:0%
<i> so scientists tried
 to observe the atoms.</i>

00:06:58.016 --> 00:07:01.187 line:0%
<i> But when they pointed
 a detector to track the atoms,</i>

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.189 line:0%
<i> they changed
 their coordinates,</i>

00:07:03.222 --> 00:07:05.558 line:0%
<i> meaning atoms
 can act like both waves</i>

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.726 line:0%
<i> and particles when in motion.</i>

00:07:07.759 --> 00:07:10.729 line:0%
<i> To this day, scientists have
 still no clue why,</i>

00:07:10.762 --> 00:07:13.732 line:0%
<i> but just observing
 your atoms in motion</i>

00:07:13.765 --> 00:07:15.901 line:0%
<i> can change their destination,</i>

00:07:15.934 --> 00:07:19.839 line:0%
<i> which would be
 really bad for you,
 the person teleporting.</i>

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:21.640 align:start position:0%
It's like when
you're playing "Mario Bros"

00:07:21.673 --> 00:07:24.076 align:start position:0%
and you look at the Boo
and it stops,

00:07:24.109 --> 00:07:26.579 align:start position:0%
and then you turn around
and it chases you.

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:28.914 align:start size:88% position:13%
The observation of it
definitely changes behavior.

00:07:28.947 --> 00:07:30.549 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Wow, that--
- Yeah.

00:07:30.582 --> 00:07:33.819
That does hurt
my brain a little bit.

00:07:33.852 --> 00:07:35.254
<i> So maybe we're still
 very far away</i>

00:07:35.287 --> 00:07:38.090
<i> from actually being
 able to teleport,</i>

00:07:38.123 --> 00:07:41.093
<i> but what if we could achieve
 almost the same experience</i>

00:07:41.126 --> 00:07:43.028
<i> without even
 leaving our homes?</i>

00:07:43.061 --> 00:07:45.998
Narrator:
<i> By strapping on 3D
 visual display goggles,</i>

00:07:46.031 --> 00:07:48.934
<i> one can be immersed
 into new worlds.</i>

00:07:48.967 --> 00:07:51.804
The experience
of virtual reality

00:07:51.837 --> 00:07:54.940
<i> is similar to teleportation
 in that your body,</i>

00:07:54.973 --> 00:07:58.511
<i> your consciousness,
 your awareness
 was somewhere else,</i>

00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:02.548
+and instantaneously
you are in a completely
different environment.

00:08:02.581 --> 00:08:04.183
Marques:
<i> All right, let's talk about
 the history of VR.</i>

00:08:04.216 --> 00:08:06.151
How did this whole
VR thing start?

00:08:06.184 --> 00:08:10.022
You get into the '80s and '90s
and you get the Eye Phone.

00:08:10.055 --> 00:08:12.258
No, not that iPhone.

00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:15.828
This was the "eye,"
so, E-Y-E Phone.

00:08:15.861 --> 00:08:17.730
You had an individual
named Jaron Lanier

00:08:17.763 --> 00:08:21.133 line:0%
<i> who wanted to create
 a new type of instrument.</i>

00:08:21.166 --> 00:08:23.802 line:0%
These are the special glasses
called the Eye Phones.

00:08:23.835 --> 00:08:26.872
When you put them on,
you're seeing inside
an imaginary world

00:08:26.905 --> 00:08:28.841
instead of inside
the physical world.

00:08:28.874 --> 00:08:30.643
Greg:
<i> It was a giant headset.</i>

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:32.811
A huge computer had to run it.
It cost a ton of money.

00:08:32.844 --> 00:08:35.648
<i> But what you got
 was a virtual world</i>

00:08:35.681 --> 00:08:39.118
<i> that responded to your touch
 and what you saw.</i>

00:08:39.151 --> 00:08:42.121
Blake:
<i> It was really the Eye Phone
 that helped introduce</i>

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:43.822
<i> virtual reality as a product</i>

00:08:43.855 --> 00:08:47.660
that you might have in
your lifetime to the masses.

00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:51.030
<i> Throughout the '90s,
 video games became more
 and more realistic,</i>

00:08:51.063 --> 00:08:54.266
<i> so that became
 a great first frontier
 for virtual reality to exist.</i>

00:08:54.299 --> 00:08:57.670
<i> In my mall, they had these
 VR arcade experiences,</i>

00:08:57.703 --> 00:08:59.905
<i> like "Dactyl Nightmare."</i>

00:08:59.938 --> 00:09:02.274
Reporter:
<i> Truly cutting-edge technology,
 but to my eyes,</i>

00:09:02.307 --> 00:09:04.043
<i> these units still have
 a way to go.</i>

00:09:06.144 --> 00:09:09.148
- But there was a problem.
- It was making people sick.

00:09:09.181 --> 00:09:11.684
Headaches, nausea, sweats.

00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:14.053 align:start size:81% position:19%
- ( woman laughing )
- Oh, my God!

00:09:14.086 --> 00:09:15.854
- I can see seagulls.
- Okay, it's broken.

00:09:15.887 --> 00:09:17.823
Blake:<i> All types of things</i>

00:09:17.856 --> 00:09:19.325
<i> that are connected
 to motion sickness.</i>

00:09:19.358 --> 00:09:21.260
What early VR did have though,

00:09:21.293 --> 00:09:23.862
<i> was that it looked futuristic.</i>

00:09:23.895 --> 00:09:25.297
<i> It looked super high tech.</i>

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:27.967
<i> None of them
 actually did much,</i>

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.336
but they looked cool
when your friends came over.

00:09:30.369 --> 00:09:33.038
Marques:
<i> So, despite the technology
 being far from perfect,</i>

00:09:33.071 --> 00:09:35.741
<i> virtual reality headsets
 promised to immediately</i>

00:09:35.774 --> 00:09:38.711
<i> transport us
 to another world.</i>

00:09:38.744 --> 00:09:41.146
<i> So, today I'm joined
 by Jake from Game Ranks</i>

00:09:41.179 --> 00:09:43.148
<i> and we're gonna test out
 some of the retro VR</i>

00:09:43.181 --> 00:09:45.284
<i> and maybe get a little dizzy
 in the process.</i>

00:09:45.317 --> 00:09:47.753 align:start position:0%
This is Dope Or Nope.

00:09:50.389 --> 00:09:52.625 align:start size:94% position:6%
- First of all,
  Jake, welcome back.
- Hey, thanks for having me.

00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:54.193 align:start position:0% line:0%
Season two.
Love when we have people back.

00:09:54.226 --> 00:09:58.130
We got this first thing
coming to us from the year 1993.

00:09:58.163 --> 00:09:59.932 align:start size:63% position:38%
I'm ready to
get jacked in, so...

00:09:59.965 --> 00:10:02.167
For sure.
Let's plug ourselves in.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:05.237 size:88% position:44%
