<i> and most at this point had
 given up on the VR industry.</i>

00:14:53.458 --> 00:14:56.261
The year is 2012,
not that long ago.

00:14:56.294 --> 00:14:58.931
<i> But it was long ago enough
 that people thought</i>

00:14:58.964 --> 00:15:00.666
virtual reality was a joke,
it was a punch line.

00:15:00.699 --> 00:15:03.368
We all thought it was done.
It made you nauseous.

00:15:03.401 --> 00:15:06.371
But there were a few people
that still loved VR,

00:15:06.404 --> 00:15:08.941
and one of them was a teenager
named Palmer Lucky.

00:15:08.974 --> 00:15:11.143 line:0%
Virtual reality,
people have been wanting it
for a really long time.

00:15:11.176 --> 00:15:13.078 line:0%
We finally have the technology
to make it happen.

00:15:13.111 --> 00:15:16.548
It used gyroscopes,
accelerometers, you know,

00:15:16.581 --> 00:15:20.485
sensors that we know very well
from the Eye Phone,

00:15:20.518 --> 00:15:22.521
and the latency was super low.

00:15:22.554 --> 00:15:24.256
Blake:<i> The sensors,</i>

00:15:24.289 --> 00:15:26.091
<i> they're able to so quickly
 calculate in real time</i>

00:15:26.124 --> 00:15:27.559
<i> what should be there
 when you look that way,</i>

00:15:27.592 --> 00:15:29.227
<i> and that's what really
 tricked your brain</i>

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:30.495
into thinking
that you're somewhere else.

00:15:30.528 --> 00:15:32.130
All of a sudden,
we thought for a second,

00:15:32.163 --> 00:15:34.399
"Hey, this might be
a thing again

00:15:34.432 --> 00:15:36.201
<i> because of head tracking."</i>

00:15:36.234 --> 00:15:38.070
And it was this idea
that just by putting on

00:15:38.103 --> 00:15:39.571
<i> this pair of glasses,
 these goggles,</i>

00:15:39.604 --> 00:15:41.173
<i> you could be anywhere.</i>

00:15:41.206 --> 00:15:43.342
Really bringing it back
to teleportation.

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:49.081
A lot of times with technology,
we're not even conceiving

00:15:49.114 --> 00:15:51.350
of something that's
just about to arrive.

00:15:51.383 --> 00:15:53.685 line:0%
<i> The day before
 the iPhone was announced,</i>

00:15:53.718 --> 00:15:58.023 line:0%
<i> we didn't even conceive
 that a phone was able
 to do those things.</i>

00:15:58.056 --> 00:16:02.260
So who knows?
Maybe someone is working
on teleportation right now

00:16:02.293 --> 00:16:04.330
and we're gonna have
the ability to teleport.

00:16:06.398 --> 00:16:08.200
Marques:<i> So, back to
 the original question.</i>

00:16:08.233 --> 00:16:11.536
<i> Is teleportation technology
 closer than we think?</i>

00:16:11.569 --> 00:16:13.739
<i> Well, to find out, I'm gonna
 speak with astrophysicist</i>

00:16:13.772 --> 00:16:16.742
<i> and all-around great guy
 Dr. Neil deGrasse Tyson,</i>

00:16:16.775 --> 00:16:19.311
<i> and hopefully he can
 shed some light on this</i>

00:16:19.344 --> 00:16:22.147
<i>and tell us if we're any closer
 to actually beaming up.</i>

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:25.117 line:0%
Neil deGrasse Tyson,
thank you for joining me.

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:27.753 line:0%
Would it be, do you think,
ever physically possible

00:16:27.786 --> 00:16:30.555
to build this idea
we've imagined

00:16:30.588 --> 00:16:32.424
of the teleportation machine?

00:16:32.457 --> 00:16:35.560
I think central
to the teleportation machine

00:16:35.593 --> 00:16:38.397
is the memory

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:41.500
of how all of your molecules
are configured.

00:16:41.533 --> 00:16:45.370
It's an information
processing challenge.

00:16:45.403 --> 00:16:47.172
I can turn you into energy

00:16:47.205 --> 00:16:49.307
and then move that energy
to the other place

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.610
and then convert that energy
back into particles.

00:16:51.643 --> 00:16:53.478
But I have to remember,

00:16:53.511 --> 00:16:56.648
I need the instruction set
to reassemble you.

00:16:56.681 --> 00:16:58.316
Because once
it becomes energy,

00:16:58.349 --> 00:17:00.318
you've lost all
of that information.

00:17:00.351 --> 00:17:02.487
So, have we ever converted
anything to energy?

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:04.756
Yeah, we do that in particle
accelerators all the time.

00:17:04.789 --> 00:17:08.160
Like, you can
reconstruct the same shape
in a different place,

00:17:08.193 --> 00:17:13.231
but remembering where each
molecule is inside that shape?

00:17:13.264 --> 00:17:17.335
So, let's go to a "Star Trek"
style teleportation.

00:17:17.368 --> 00:17:19.638
So, it disassembles
your molecules,

00:17:19.671 --> 00:17:21.106
<i> turns you into
 a beam of energy,</i>

00:17:21.139 --> 00:17:23.175
<i> reassembles it
 on the other side.</i>

00:17:23.208 --> 00:17:25.610
All right,
so is that still you?

00:17:25.643 --> 00:17:29.548
I have to say no.
It is a copy of you.

00:17:29.581 --> 00:17:33.819
<i> And basically you just
 divided yourself into two--
 basically cloning.</i>

00:17:33.852 --> 00:17:36.421
<i> Now, how do we turn
 that into you?</i>

00:17:36.454 --> 00:17:41.193
What we'd need to do is
not simply copy the molecules,

00:17:41.226 --> 00:17:45.530
you have to copy
the neurosynaptic memories

00:17:45.563 --> 00:17:49.367
<i> that you carry
 in your brain's molecules.</i>

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:52.537
<i> All right, so, boom!
 It disappears here,
 shows up over there.</i>

00:17:52.570 --> 00:17:56.575
<i> It is there instantly,
 so that all your particles</i>

00:17:56.608 --> 00:17:58.410
<i> will disappear
 simultaneously</i>

00:17:58.443 --> 00:18:00.745
<i> and then show up
 in another place.</i>

00:18:00.778 --> 00:18:05.217
I don't know how
to teleport memories

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:10.322
that exist in the static
molecules of your brain.

00:18:10.355 --> 00:18:13.859
If you could do that,
then I'm convinced

00:18:13.892 --> 00:18:16.361
we are moving you
from one location to another.

00:18:16.394 --> 00:18:20.732
Do you think if virtual reality 
can get good enough

00:18:20.765 --> 00:18:25.570
that maybe that's enough
to satisfy that part
of our curiosity

00:18:25.603 --> 00:18:28.240
where we want to transport
just to see cool things?

00:18:28.273 --> 00:18:29.774
We'll just sort of put
the headset on and be there.

00:18:29.807 --> 00:18:31.910
Yes, we've taken baby steps
in that direction.

00:18:31.943 --> 00:18:35.247
<i> But if you could smell the air
 and feel the breeze</i>

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:38.416
<i> and watch the birds
 fly over it,</i>

00:18:38.449 --> 00:18:39.851
<i> maybe that's sufficient.</i>

00:18:39.884 --> 00:18:43.588
<i> You don't know
 whether you are looking</i>

00:18:43.621 --> 00:18:45.590
<i> at an image or reality.</i>

00:18:45.623 --> 00:18:47.592
<i> At that point,
 I don't think it matters</i>

00:18:47.625 --> 00:18:49.861
<i> whether you actually go
 to the Grand Canyon.</i>

00:18:49.894 --> 00:18:51.696
<i> And then you can just change
 where your location is.</i>

00:18:51.729 --> 00:18:55.267
But does it matter
that it's real?

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:56.568
That's a different question.

00:18:56.601 --> 00:18:59.604
The knowledge that it's real

00:18:59.637 --> 00:19:01.873
seems to matter to us.

00:19:01.906 --> 00:19:04.609
You know, what is real?

00:19:04.642 --> 00:19:06.545
I sound like Morpheus.

00:19:06.578 --> 00:19:07.612 line:0%
<i> ( Neil imitating Morpheus )
 What is real?</i>

00:19:07.645 --> 00:19:10.215 line:0%
<i> How do you define real?</i>

00:19:10.248 --> 00:19:13.318
If your brain is everything,
then traveling doesn't matter.

00:19:13.351 --> 00:19:16.221
We'll just inject it in you.
That could come.

00:19:16.254 --> 00:19:18.290
Well, that's really deep.
Thank you again.

00:19:18.323 --> 00:19:19.624 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I love your shirt.
- Oh, thank you.

